<p>This describes how to correct the hue of photos taken with a digital 
 camera. If there are extreme light and shade differences between the object 
 and background, the object may be too dark or too light. This function 
 is effective for those types of photos.</p>
<p class="Note">Note:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>This function is available only with color printers.</p></li>
<li><p>This function may not be effective for all image 
 data.</p></li>
</ul>
<h2>Exposure</h2>
<p>Exposure refers to the levels of light in which the photo was taken. 
 If there are extreme light and shade differences between the object and background, 
 the object may be under exposed (too dark) or over exposed (too light).</p>
<ol>
<li><p>Display photo data taken with a digital camera 
 in an application.</p></li>
<li><p>Open the printing preferences dialog box.</p></li>
<li><p>Click the <span class="Term-Soft">Print Quality:Advanced</span> 
 menu on the <span class="Term-Soft">Detailed Settings</span> tab.</p></li>
<li><p>Select the <span class="Term-Soft">Adjust Photographs</span> 
 check box.</p></li>
<li><p>After making any other necessary settings, click 
 <span class="Term-OS">OK</span> to close the printing preferences dialog 
 box.</p></li>
<li><p>Start printing.</p></li>
</ol>
